## Solution

The matrix $A_n$ has entries $a_{ij} = \cos\big((i-1)n + j\big)$ for $1 \le i, j \le n$.

**Key observation.** Apply the addition formula $\cos(\alpha+\beta) = \cos\alpha\cos\beta - \sin\alpha\sin\beta$ with $\alpha = (i-1)n$ and $\beta = j$:

$$a_{ij} = \cos\big((i-1)n\big)\cos(j) \;-\; \sin\big((i-1)n\big)\sin(j).$$

Define the column vectors
$$\mathbf{c} = \big(\cos 0,\; \cos n,\; \cos 2n,\; \dots,\; \cos(n-1)n\big)^T, \quad \mathbf{s} = \big(\sin 0,\; \sin n,\; \sin 2n,\; \dots,\; \sin(n-1)n\big)^T,$$
and the column vectors
$$\mathbf{v} = \big(\cos 1,\; \cos 2,\; \dots,\; \cos n\big)^T, \quad \mathbf{w} = \big(\sin 1,\; \sin 2,\; \dots,\; \sin n\big)^T.$$

Then the entire matrix factors as
$$A_n = \mathbf{c}\,\mathbf{v}^T \;-\; \mathbf{s}\,\mathbf{w}^T.$$

This is the sum of two **rank‑1** matrices, so
$$\operatorname{rank}(A_n) \le 2.$$

For every $n \ge 3$, the rank is at most $2 < n$, hence $A_n$ is singular and
$$d_n = \det(A_n) = 0 \qquad \text{for all } n \ge 3.$$

**Verification for $n=3$.** Row $i$ uses $\alpha=(i-1)\cdot 3$: row 1 gives $\cos(0+j)=\cos j$, row 2 gives $\cos(3+j)$, row 3 gives $\cos(6+j)$, matching $\cos 1,\dots,\cos 9$. The factorization shows $d_3=0$. ✓

Since $d_n = 0$ for all $n \ge 3$, the sequence is eventually constant (equal to $0$), and therefore

$$\boxed{\lim_{n\to\infty} d_n = 0}.$$

